.TH LIBMPQ__UPDATE_COMPACT_EX 3 "2026-11-16" "libmpq 0.9.0" "libmpq Library Functions"
.SH NAME
libmpq__update_compact_ex \- compact with externally known archive filenames
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <libmpq/mpq.h>

.BI "int32_t libmpq__update_compact_ex("
.BI "        mpq_update_s *" "update,"
.BI "        const char *const *" "filenames,"
.BI "        size_t " "filename_count"
.BI ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Immediately compact the current private transaction stage using the same
physical planner, packed writer, validation and adoption as
.BR libmpq__update_compact (3).
Commit alone publishes; this call does not consume the update handle.
No minimum archive size or reduction in total bytes is guaranteed.
.PP
The array and every NUL-terminated string are borrowed only for the synchronous
call. Zero count is identical to ordinary compaction and does not dereference
filenames, whether NULL or non-NULL. Nonzero input requires a non-NULL array
and non-NULL, nonempty entries. Counts and merged ownership capacities must
fit the existing private uint32_t counters and pointer-array allocation sizes.
.PP
Every supplied spelling must resolve through strict current-stage identity
validation before a candidate is constructed. Missing entries are errors;
they are not silently ignored or replaced by another spelling or identity.
Valid case variants and exact-byte duplicates are accepted. Distinct classic
slash spellings are not normalized. Localized-only names and all matching
locale/platform variants are preserved. Full, unambiguous BET-only identities
are accepted without fabricated classic tuples; reduced or contradictory
provenance is rejected.
.PP
Names augment transaction-known spellings and the existing internal listfile.
They do not create or add entries to that listfile. All live references to a
shared encrypted payload need sufficient compatible key provenance. Different
aliases can share one payload when their basename-derived keys agree; no alias
is discarded, detached or duplicated to make compaction succeed. Valid names
alone do not guarantee that the layout is reconstructible or the plaintext
correct. The layout and trailer checks in libmpq__update_compact(3), including
its v2 declared-extent check, also apply here. KEY adjustment uses MPQ-relative
member position and uncompressed size, excluding any embedded prefix.
.PP
After actual candidate validation, all still-valid supplied spellings are
revalidated and copied into replacement transaction-owned knowledge before
adoption. Ownership transfers only after successful adoption. No caller
pointers, old canonical numbers or tuple indices are retained. Distinct aliases
are retained, not only one representative per physical payload.
.PP
A proven already-compact archive still validates every supplied entry and all
existing compaction eligibility. It returns without creating a candidate,
rewriting bytes, invalidating signatures, changing the working path, changing
dirty state or retaining new names. A later call may require the same names
again. A prior mutation's dirty state is preserved.
.PP
Semantic rejection and pre-adoption failures preserve the previous valid stage
and filename knowledge. All temporary name allocations are released. Existing
qualifying candidate-adoption errors may leave the transaction abort-only;
there is no new recovery or publication guarantee. Abort and failed publication
retain the existing original-source guarantees. Ordinary clean-commit behavior
and ordinary named-update alias restrictions are unchanged. Compact last when
final layout matters; later supported mutations may introduce gaps.
.PP
Supported embedded prefix/user-data preservation, MPQE publication, signatures,
raw integrity, attributes and bounded payload copying follow ordinary
compaction. Budget disk space for the working stage and candidate, with the
existing extra embedded/MPQE temporaries where applicable.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
/* Both names identify existing members in sample.mpq. */
int32_t
compact_with_known_names(void)
{
    const char *const filenames[] = {
        "docs/sample.txt",
        "data/sample.bin"
    };
    const size_t filename_count = sizeof(filenames) / sizeof(filenames[0]);
    mpq_update_s *update = NULL;
    int32_t status = libmpq__update_begin(&update, "sample.mpq");
    if (status != 0)
        return status;

    status = libmpq__update_compact_ex(update, filenames, filename_count);
    if (status != 0) {
        (void)libmpq__update_abort(update);
        return status; /* Abort consumed the handle. Keep the first error. */
    }

    /* Commit publishes the stage and consumes update even on error. */
    return libmpq__update_commit(update);
}
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns 0 for a validated compaction or no-op, or a negative libmpq error.
.SH ERRORS
LIBMPQ_ERROR_EXIST reports an invalid/abort-only update, a NULL array with
nonzero count, a NULL/empty entry, or an absent filename. LIBMPQ_ERROR_FORMAT
reports ambiguous/reduced/contradictory identity provenance, incompatible shared
keys or unsupported layout/metadata. LIBMPQ_ERROR_DECRYPT reports unavailable
required key provenance. LIBMPQ_ERROR_SIZE reports unrepresentable capacity,
output sizes or positions.
LIBMPQ_ERROR_MALLOC reports allocation failure. Existing open, read, write,
seek and close errors propagate. No entry from a failed batch is installed.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR libmpq (3),
.BR libmpq__update_compact (3),
.BR libmpq__update_begin (3),
.BR libmpq__update_commit (3),
.BR libmpq__update_abort (3),
.BR libmpq__file_identity (3)
